## Deterministic Cleanup with Drop

**Drop** is the automatic cleanup that happens when a value goes out of scope. It frees memory, closes files, and releases resources — deterministically, at predictable points.

### When Drop Happens

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let v = Vec[Int]();  // Vec allocated
  v.push(1);
  v.push(2);
  return 0;
  // v goes out of scope — Vec is DROPPED
  // All heap memory freed, no leaks
}
```

### Drop is Deterministic

Unlike garbage-collected languages, you know exactly when cleanup happens:

```xiom
fn process() -> Unit {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(42);
  // data dropped here — always, no exceptions, no GC cycles
}
```

### Drop for Multiple Values

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let a = Vec[Int]();
  a.push(1);
  let b = Vec[Int]();
  b.push(2);
  let c = Vec[Int]();
  c.push(3);
  return 0;
  // Drop order: c (last created), then b, then a (first created)
}
```

### Drop After Move

A moved value is NOT dropped — the new owner handles cleanup:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(42);
  let v2 = v;  // ownership moves to v2
  // v is NOT dropped (it's dead after move)
  return 0;
  // v2 is dropped — Vec freed once
}
```

### Drop Guarantees

- Every value is dropped exactly once
- No double-free: the compiler proves it
- No leaks: all owned memory is freed
- Even on early returns or panics, drop runs properly

